Z||000000||Philphine||03-22-2001||12:12 PM||||<BR>anyone have enough experience with the various types to know if one might be best if you are using a top mount battery? i hate side mounts and change them whenever i need to replace or get a battery.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||03-22-2001||07:06 PM||||I personally can't stand top post batteries.  They get corrosion much too easy, and even if you're careful to coat it with an anti-corrosive spray, it still manages to go bad after a while.  Also, the top mount bolts can spin in the (lead)metal if over tightened or worn.  The threaded area of a top mount never seems to last more than about 5 years also, due to corrosion and rust.  Side mounts have much less corrosion and the bolts rarely strip.  If the bolt head gets ruined, they're still easy to get a pair of pliers on.  The only problem I have with side mount batteries are the places the car companies put them! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  They were only used in cars with limited space originally, but now everything uses them.  Seems like the companies really like to cram the battery in some painful places on Jeeps, most cars, and mini-vans.  Well...that's my $.02<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>P.S.  I bought an Optima battery (gel cell)for a race car a couple years ago, and after the season used it in several cars.  It has both top posts and side posts.  It was an awesome battery, so I converted my VW bug to side posts to use it and avoid the possibility of the dreaded "seat spring to battery post" fire, and covered the top posts with a rubber mat <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
